  "types_with_bad_inspect_methods": ["game-task-event", "game-task-control"],
  // (method 82 rigid-body-object): UNHELD (rigid-body rung, second target).
  // Widened to the full four-arg attack-handler shape (process-drawable
  // attack-info touching-shapes-entry penetrate) and construction-obs-h.gc's
  // own override and tail-call updated in the same change; both objects
  // decode clean under the new signature.
  // (method 210 wvehicle), (method 211 wvehicle), (method 62 wvehicle) and
  // (method 216 wvehicle): a related cluster of debug/network telemetry
  // methods, most writing into a shared fixed scratchpad address
  // (0x70002000) at offsets past 500 bytes, arg1/arg2 typed "object"
  // throughout since neither this rung's own scoped decode nor a quick
  // corpus check turned up a declared type wide enough to cover the
  // observed offsets (528+ in method 210 alone). (method 62 wvehicle) also
  // reads a plain float off wvehicle's own self pointer at offset 720, a
  // field this rung's own scoped decode of wvehicle-physics.gc did not
  // otherwise need and wvehicle's own field list does not yet declare in
  // that region. Resolving the scratchpad buffer's real layout and
  // wvehicle's own field gap are both bigger derivations than this rung's
  // band covers (rigid-body-small-tier rung, held; see the report).
  // (method 212 wvehicle) forwards the same scratchpad constant into
  // (method 211 wvehicle) and is landed with a narrow type_casts.jsonc
  // entry instead, since its own failing ops are a single self-contained
  // quad copy, not part of the wider buffer-layout gap.
  // (method 249 wvehicle), (method 236 wvehicle) and (method 235 wvehicle):
  // the same class as the wvehicle debug/telemetry cluster above, each its
  // own multi-vector stack (and, for method 249, a second fixed scratchpad
  // region at 0x70003000 distinct from 0x70002000) whose full layout is a
  // bigger derivation than this rung's band covers (rigid-body-small-tier
  // rung, held; see the report).
  // rigid-body-control-integrator rung: (method 9 rigid-body-control),
  // (method 62 collide-shape-moving) (jak3's own rbody-collision), (method
  // 14 rigid-body-control), (method 25 rigid-body-control) and (method 9
  // rigid-body-info), all previously held by the rigid-body rung, decode
  // clean once un-held: method 9's own "expression building failed part way
  // through" was the decompiler needing full type analysis to structure the
  // "(and (begin lin-check) (begin ang-check)) -> zero four vectors" branch
  // and resolve rbody-collision's own signature, not an unfixable wall; it
  // has NO jak3 twin for that near-rest zeroing block or for dropping jak3's
  // own "flags enable-collision ? rbody-collision : method-14" branch down
  // to an unconditional rbody-collision call, see the landed method's own
  // comment. Method 62 needed a type_casts.jsonc cast of its own s5 register
  // to rigid-body-move-work (the fixed #x70002000 scratchpad, confirmed
  // field-for-field), a stack_structures.jsonc entry for a local 2-vector
  // array, a cast for a *rigid-body-surfaces* array-index pointer, and check-
  // vehicle-line-spheres-hack's own extern signature (still held itself, see
  // below) plus (method 25 rigid-body-control)'s own signature so both
  // callees' return types stop poisoning method 62's own type propagation.
  // Method 14's "Failed store" (a local stack quaternion address computed as
  // a plain pointer offset) needed a type_casts.jsonc range-cast to
  // "(pointer uint128)" (a single-op cast left it unresolved; the range form
  // is what actually took). Method 25's own body needed a stack_structures
  // entry (sp+16, a local rigid-body-impact scratch copy) once its signature
  // was corrected from the rigid-body rung's bare "()" guess to (rigid-body-
  // control rigid-body-impact) with a float return, proven by (method 62
  // collide-shape-moving)'s own call site. (method 9 rigid-body-info) needed
  // a type_casts.jsonc cast on the loop-index pointer arithmetic (this +
  // v1*20, reading the three inv-inertial-tensor diagonal entries at +112)
  // to "(pointer float)".
  //
  // Eight remain held. (method 34 rigid-body-control) and (method 35 rigid-
  // body-control) are documented at their own :methods entries in
  // all-types.gc (unresolved arg shape past 3-4 registers; method 34's own
  // call into method 35 passes only a3 explicitly, leaving a1/a2 unclear).
  // apply-overlap-penalty-forces, ray-tube-intersect, check-vehicle-line-
  // spheres-hack and collide-sphere-with-line-sphere-hack are standalone
  // loop-heavy helpers ("CFG building failed: Could not fully resolve CFG"
  // for the first three) the resurrected rb-penalty-forces-work/ray-tube-
  // intersect-params/hack-line-sphere/check-line-spheres-hack-work types
  // alone were not enough to unblock; check-vehicle-line-spheres-hack's own
  // extern signature IS now active (collide-shape-moving check-line-spheres-
  // hack-params none, proven by (method 62 collide-shape-moving)'s own call
  // site), unblocking that caller, but the callee's own CFG failure is
  // untouched. debug-draw-rigid-body-move (the debug-only companion to
  // debug-rigid-body-move, itself landed) has the same "unknown type" CFG
  // failure. (method 66 collide-shape-moving) is jak3's own collide-with-
  // all-collide-cache-prims by slot position (jak3 67, shifted down one for
  // jakx deleting react-to-pat!, confirmed by the same offset(N)=16+4*N
  // machine-code math that pinned method 62); its raw disassembly matches
  // jak3's own block-for-block goto/label shape ("CFG building failed:
  // Could not fully resolve CFG" is the same class jak3's OWN decompile hit,
  // hence jak3's landed source keeps explicit cfg-N labels rather than a
  // structured loop), but jakx's own machine code dispatches the mesh/sphere
  // collision test through a VIRTUAL METHOD on the root prim's own vtable at
  // machine offset 76 (method 15 on whatever collide-shape-prim subtype),
  // where jak3's source instead branches on prim-type's sign to two named
  // free functions (collide-with-collide-cache-prim-mesh/-sphere); this is a
  // confirmed op-level difference, not a config gap, and hand-carrying it
  // safely needs that vtable slot's own real signature nailed down first.
  // (method 62 collide-shape-moving), defined in the same file, was NOT
  // held: it already decodes clean under its own existing type.
  //
  // wvehicle-core rung: twelve more held in wvehicle.gc itself, none of
  // them the four named-target methods (214/215/218/219) this rung lands.
  // plot-engine-torque-curve: debug-only (behind *debug-segment*, only
  // called when it is #t), its own sp+16 scratch mixes four vectors with
